[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the outage. PC11 (172.16.20.11) is currently on the network and communicating normally. From PC10 and PC9 (same VLAN 20), pings to PC11 succeed with 0% loss, and PC11 can ping back both neighbors and its default gateway 172.16.20.254 with low latency. On PC11, the NIC is UP with IP 172.16.20.11/24 and a correct default route via 172.16.20.254. On the access switch (switch2), the access port for PC11 (eth5, access_vlan20_port6) is UP and bridged into br_vlan20, as expected, and other VLAN 20 hosts are also reachable. 

Mechanism: If PC11 were “off the network,” we would see failed ARP/ICMP between same-VLAN neighbors and no reachability to the gateway; instead, ARP/ICMP work in both directions, indicating healthy L2 and L3 paths. Localization: No faults observed on switch2’s VLAN 20 bridge or PC11’s interface/route. 

Root cause: Not reproducible; the original issue appears to have been transient or already corrected on the host or cabling prior to this check.

Corrective fix: No network changes are required. Do not move the port. Ask the user to confirm service is restored. If the problem recurs, investigate the workstation side (NIC power-save/duplex flaps, local firewall, or cable) and collect timestamps so we can correlate with switch port logs for intermittent link events. 
=== END ===